      "title": "netscaler: Fix absolute time when saving v2.0-v2.2, at least when not seeking",
      "message": "netscaler: Fix absolute time when saving v2.0-v2.2, at least when not seeking\n\nFor the Netscaler v2.0 file type, correctly write the absolute time to the\n32-bit absolute time field, instead of to the relative time field. Also,\nthe size of the absolute time in v2.0-v2.2 Netscaler files is 32-bit, so\nfix that (which affects the memcpy when running on Big Endian\narchitecture.)\n\nOn versions 2.3 and later, this was written incorrectly as well but a more\nprecise 8 octet absolute time is used instead when reading.\n\nCoverity CID 1499452\n\nFixup c317a1c3cdee6c173038dc28e60c2199803dd6a3\n\nThis fixes saving from editcap, one-pass TShark, etc. For Export\nSpecified Packets from Wireshark and two-pass TShark, this makes the\nabsolute timestamps 0 instead of random, due to a different issue where\nfor Netscaler before v2.3 (including v1) timestamps in the packet are\nrelative and only available in the wtap_rec from libwiretap on a\nsequential read, not when using wtap_seek_read.\n\nThat could possibly be fixed by adding the timestamp back from the\nframe data, where it is saved for this reason and made available\nto the packet_info struct (cf. epan/packet.c dissect_record()).\nAdd some comments in the relevant places in tshark.c and file.c\nabout that.\n",
      "author_name": "John Thacker",
      "author_email": "johnthacker@gmail.com",
